WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:09.050
Hi, I'm Gena.
I'm here with Charlie Brooker,

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.260
creator of Black Mirror,

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.178
and these are his Netflix IX.

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:15.807
So where do ideas usually
come to you?

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:20.478
Ideas tend to spring up from
a conversation,

00:00:20.478 --> 00:00:22.981
- Okay.
- So usually, it's

00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:26.192
a conversation where I'm having an idea
that strikes me as funny.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.861
- Right.
- So it's a sort of "what if" idea.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:30.071
What if, you know,

00:00:30.071 --> 00:00:31.698
toast became sentient, and -

00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:33.658
- A better one than that.
- Okay.

00:00:33.658 --> 00:00:36.369
So that's usually it.
Sometimes...

00:00:37.078 --> 00:00:39.414
I've now turned into the sort
of person who goes running,

00:00:39.414 --> 00:00:41.791
- every now and then,
- Oh, alright.

00:00:41.791 --> 00:00:44.419
which young me would've
thought was disgusting

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:46.546
So once I was running, and

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:49.215
Belinda Carlisle - 'Heaven is
a Place on Earth' came on,

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.551
and then I thought
"Ooh, that's perfect

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:54.554
for 'San Junipero'", which I was
writing at the time.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:57.098
So that's sort of
why that went in.

00:00:57.432 --> 00:01:00.477
So if you could choose
any other career in the world

00:01:00.477 --> 00:01:01.770
right, you're not doing TV writing,

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.397
so you can do anything,
astronaut, zookeeper,

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:07.567
uh... creator of sentient
toast people.

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:09.152
- Yes.
- What would you be doing?

00:01:09.152 --> 00:01:10.111
Serial killing.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:12.906
No um... no I don't know.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.283
I was like, "Uhhh..."

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:18.495
Not really - although I think
I'd probably be quite well suited for that.

00:01:18.495 --> 00:01:18.953
Yeah!

00:01:19.496 --> 00:01:22.415
- My first job was cartoonist.
- Okay.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:25.210
And I sort of stopped
doing it because

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.170
a.) I wasn't a very
good cartoonist,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:31.007
and b.) the speech bubbles
drove me up the wall.

00:01:31.007 --> 00:01:34.803
It was so boring,
having to do all the text by hand.

00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:38.264
And of course these days,
you'd just use a computer to do that,

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:40.433
so maybe I'd go back into being
a cartoonist.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.310
You could probably still do it
under a different name. 

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.229
I could do it under
my own name!

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:45.897
- Oh that's true!
- I don't have to change my name!

00:01:45.897 --> 00:01:48.191
- Well, it depends what I'm drawing.
- Move country!

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:53.196
If you were shrunk
to the size of a Malteser -

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:53.863
Yup.

00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:56.199
- and then trapped in TV,

00:01:56.199 --> 00:01:58.201
- like in Willy Wonka, with Mike Teavee, right?
- Yup.

00:01:58.201 --> 00:01:59.911
What TV show would you choose?

00:01:59.911 --> 00:02:01.412
Why do I have to be little in it?

00:02:01.412 --> 00:02:05.083
I just feel like it makes more sense.
I was just thinking of the Willy Wonka parallel.

00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:06.417
So you're shrunk to the size of
a Malteser,

00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:08.253
You can't be big.
Malteser-sized.

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:09.754
But in TV you're fine.

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:10.922
I'm normal -
So hang on,

00:02:10.922 --> 00:02:13.258
So in the world of the show
I'm normal-sized?

00:02:13.258 --> 00:02:16.010
Yeah, but in real life you are
Malteser-sized.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.555
But these days TVs are so big
I might be more like -

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:20.098
You're still a Malteser,
don't ruin the question.

00:02:20.098 --> 00:02:22.225
Okay.
I might be cat-sized.

00:02:23.184 --> 00:02:26.771
I will randomly choose
Peppa Pig.

00:02:27.772 --> 00:02:30.275
- Really?
- Because that's quite a jolly world,

00:02:30.275 --> 00:02:31.401
- Yeah, it is!
- And actually -

00:02:31.401 --> 00:02:33.736
they're all the wrong sizes,
aren't they?

00:02:33.736 --> 00:02:35.655
Because you've got - 
My wife is obsessed

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:37.574
- with the fact that they're all the wrong sizes.
- Right.

00:02:37.574 --> 00:02:39.909
You've got an elephant,
and a pig, and a mouse,

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:42.412
and they're all the same...
bloody size.

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:45.748
Is there anything
Easter-egg-wise,

00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:47.167
that fans have spotted?

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:49.460
We've sometimes put in
little gags for people

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.795
who are pressing pause.

00:02:50.795 --> 00:02:52.463
- Oh really?
- So in 'Playtest',

00:02:52.463 --> 00:02:55.508
there's a moment where he's
signing the contract,

00:02:56.384 --> 00:02:59.762
and if you pause it,
it says -

00:02:59.762 --> 00:03:01.806
I thought it'd be funny to put
in a little - 

00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:03.516
It's like The Ring, or something,
it says

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:04.934
"Now that you've paused this
and read this,

00:03:04.934 --> 00:03:07.061
you will die within
seven days."

00:03:07.061 --> 00:03:08.563
- Oh my -
- "Unless you forward the email"

00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:10.565
or something like that,
"to seven of your friends."

00:03:10.565 --> 00:03:11.941
And sure enough,
someone spotted it.

00:03:12.317 --> 00:03:14.360
Tell me your favourite episode
of Black Mirror,

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.279
if you could choose
between your children.

00:03:16.279 --> 00:03:19.199
'San Junipero' is one that I've got
a lot of affection for,

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:23.578
because it was the first one
that I wrote for Netflix,

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:28.041
and it was the first time I'd
written one that wasn't set in the UK,

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:30.835
and it was the first one with
a more optimistic tone.

00:03:30.835 --> 00:03:32.629
Do you have a favourite
from this season?

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:36.007
Ooh - again, 
it's so -

00:03:36.007 --> 00:03:38.718
There's three in this season,
they're all very, very different.

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:40.136
- So, all of them.
- Okay.

00:03:40.136 --> 00:03:41.054
Am I allowed to say
all of them?

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:41.596
Absolutely!

00:03:41.596 --> 00:03:42.931
Why didn't I just say that
at the start?

00:03:42.931 --> 00:03:43.596
I don't know!

00:03:43.596 --> 00:03:45.183
That would've been quicker.
Sorry.

00:03:45.183 --> 00:03:50.188
In 'Smithereens' it features a car
service that kind of resembles Uber.

00:03:50.188 --> 00:03:52.482
- Does it?!
- Kind of, I don't know, whatever.

00:03:53.191 --> 00:03:56.069
What is your Uber rating
and do you have a good one?

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:58.780
Well unless it's gone down
it's 4.69.

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:01.199
- 4.69?
- Is that good?

00:04:01.199 --> 00:04:01.658
Nice.

00:04:02.492 --> 00:04:03.201
What's yours?

00:04:03.534 --> 00:04:04.869
4.81.

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:06.246
That's good, isn't it?

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:09.249
That just means you're 
kowtowing to something.

00:04:09.791 --> 00:04:12.502
You do proper
human interaction,

00:04:13.127 --> 00:04:16.798
whereas I'm like a
short-term tenant

00:04:16.798 --> 00:04:18.633
in the back of that
vehicle.

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:22.220
Lucky I don't soil myself,
basically.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:23.846
Least you could do, Charlie,
mate, d'you know what I mean?

00:04:23.846 --> 00:04:26.432
If you had to choose three
TV shows that define you,

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.268
- Oh, God.
- Really sum up your personality.

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:29.268
Okay.

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:31.562
The day to day...

00:04:33.231 --> 00:04:34.774
- The Twilight Zone.
- Okay.

00:04:36.484 --> 00:04:39.654
And a last one would be...

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:42.865
[non-verbal sound]

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:47.704
And the last one would be
The Young Ones.

00:04:47.704 --> 00:04:48.538
Okay.

00:04:48.538 --> 00:04:50.456
What's your favourite
Netflix show at the moment?

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:51.456
Ooh, blimey.

00:04:51.456 --> 00:04:54.127
I'm in the middle of watching
Dead To Me, at the moment,

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:55.127
- which I'm enjoying.
- That is SO good,

00:04:55.127 --> 00:04:56.337
I'm so glad you said that.

00:04:56.337 --> 00:04:57.630
I'm enjoying that,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.215
so don't tell me what happens,
because I'm watching that.

00:04:59.215 --> 00:05:01.718
I really liked Russian Doll,
that I saw recently.

00:05:01.718 --> 00:05:04.345
I laughed a lot at -
I've seen one episode of

00:05:04.345 --> 00:05:06.556
I Think You Should Leave,
which is a sketch show,

00:05:06.556 --> 00:05:08.099
- which I thought was really funny.
- Oh yeah, that's really good!

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:10.018
I watch all the true crime
documentaries,

00:05:10.018 --> 00:05:11.519
because I'm... sick.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:15.315
If you like true crime, you should watch
Abducted In Plain Sight, just saying.

00:05:15.315 --> 00:05:17.483
Oh no, I've seen that.
That's... bananas.

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:18.860
Okay. Just... okay. Cool.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.695
In the show a lot of
young actors have

00:05:21.529 --> 00:05:23.740
I don't know...
sprung to fame,

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.034
you know, you see them in Black Mirror
and now they're humungous stars,

00:05:26.034 --> 00:05:28.369
Letitia Wright, Daniel Kaluuya
for example,

00:05:28.369 --> 00:05:31.914
is there conscious thought behind
which young talent you choose?

00:05:31.914 --> 00:05:33.666
Particularly British talent,
for example.

00:05:34.083 --> 00:05:39.714
Well, I think that's down to -
we've got a really good casting team.

00:05:39.881 --> 00:05:42.633
Daniel is a very
good example, where 

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:46.804
I remember seeing his
audition tape

00:05:46.804 --> 00:05:48.431
for 'Fifteen Million Merits',

00:05:48.431 --> 00:05:52.268
and he was amazing,
instantly amazing.

00:05:52.435 --> 00:05:57.523
And then when we shot
the episode, he does a huge speech,

00:05:57.523 --> 00:06:03.321
it was so good, I was like,
"This guy is immediately

00:06:03.321 --> 00:06:05.281
going to be a massive star,"

00:06:05.281 --> 00:06:07.116
and then quite a bit of time
seemed to go by,

00:06:07.116 --> 00:06:09.077
I was like "Where's Daniel?"
"What's happened to Daniel?"

00:06:09.077 --> 00:06:10.536
- Where's ol' Daniel?
- Where's he gone?

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:13.956
And then I remember seeing
the trail for Get Out,

00:06:13.956 --> 00:06:16.084
when that was posted online,

00:06:16.084 --> 00:06:17.251
and I was like, "YES!"

00:06:17.251 --> 00:06:18.503
Aw, that's so nice.

00:06:18.503 --> 00:06:20.588
And it was really gratifying
to hear afterwards,

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:25.009
Jordan Peele said that he'd seen
that episode of Black Mirror

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:26.594
and was like,
"Who's this guy?!"

00:06:26.594 --> 00:06:27.428
That's great.

00:06:27.428 --> 00:06:29.806
... and had got in touch with him as a result.

00:06:29.806 --> 00:06:30.806
Amazing.

00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:33.601
I don't need to do an outro,
do I? Are we good?

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:36.020
- Great.
- That was a perfect outro.

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.689
Oh sorry - we're being like,
"I'm over this."

00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:41.150
- No, I'm joking.
- "Get out."

